            <ArticleTitle>How accurate is computer-aided diagnosis?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>905-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A computer program for diagnosis of acute abdominal pain (CAD-A) was compared with an amended program (DIAG) and with clinical performance. The programs were tested at three Scottish hospitals, representing a range of practice, in 6962 cases. Accuracy of CAD-A lay in the range 48-59%, and of DIAG 56-62%. Clinical accuracy was constant at 65%. These figures suggest that computer systems based on Bayes' formula have no useful role in the diagnosis of acute abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
